# Tamsin Tealeaf's camp talks (story/camp_talk.gd). Quest: The Woman in the Locket
# (narrative/companions/locket.dialogue).

~ talk_inscription
if name:tamsin_tealeaf and quest.the_locket >= her_face and not flag.locket_inscription_read
-> inscription
endif
-> END

~ inscription
Narrator: Tamsin sits a little apart from the fire with the locket in his lap and a pick between his teeth, which is how he thinks.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: Well, aren't you a fine lock. Three months I've had you, and I've never once tried the back. You'd think I'd never met a locket.
Narrator: Something clicks. The painted woman swings aside on a hinge no wider than a hair, and behind her, worn nearly smooth by somebody's thumb, there are words.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: "For T., from S. Every morning, at the water." Oh, that's lovely. That's horrible. That's lovely and horrible.
if name:ilse_varga and flag.ilse_invitation_shown
interject name:ilse_varga: Give it here. ...Same letter. Not the same hand. My S presses hard and loops back on itself. This one's light. Like he was sure of something.
endif
interject name:silvain_aster: A betrothal token, a few centuries old. "S" is a common initial among the doomed nobility of almost everywhere. Still. "At the water" is specific. Somewhere with water she went every morning.
interject name:hedda_ironvow: Love letters are older than lies, Tamsin. Mm. They tell the truth longer, too.
* Ask him what he'll do with it. -> inscription_ask
* (Let him be.) -> inscription_end

~ inscription_ask
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: Do with it? Nothing. Keep it. Look at it. Feel terrible. It's a three-step plan and I'm very good at all three.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: ...She's not calling me, is she. She was never calling me. She was calling S, and I was the only one close enough to hear.
-> inscription_end

~ inscription_end
set locket_inscription_read
if not (quest.the_locket >= the_inscription)
quest the_locket the_inscription
endif
-> END

~ talk_coach
if name:tamsin_tealeaf and flag.locket_coach_known and not (quest.the_locket >= the_coach)
-> coach
endif
-> END

~ coach
Narrator: Tamsin hasn't touched his supper. He is turning a coin over his knuckles, end over end, and dropping it, and picking it up, and dropping it.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: A black carriage. No driver. No horses you could hear. Henrik said it the way you'd say "the weather". I robbed that carriage. Out in the world, a long way from here, on the river road.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: Nobody leaves a coach that easy. Door on the latch. Nobody on the box. Just the locket on the seat, like it was waiting. I thought I'd been lucky. I'm never that lucky.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: He put it out for me. For a thief. And I took it, and I followed the dreams all the way into his valley and right up to Ireena's door, and I showed it to her. I delivered it. I'm his postman.
* You didn't know. -> coach_didnt_know
* Then stop delivering. Decide where it goes. -> coach_decide
* (Say nothing. Hand him back his coin.) -> coach_coin

~ coach_didnt_know
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: That's the trouble with being a thief. You never know. That's the whole job. You just think you're the one doing the stealing.
-> coach_end

~ coach_decide
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: ...Decide. Yes. Thieves decide where things go. That's the only part of the job I'm actually proud of. It isn't his. It was never his.
-> coach_end

~ coach_coin
Narrator: He looks at the coin, and at you, and then he laughs, a little, the first time all evening.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: You'd make a terrible thief. You gave it back.
-> coach_end

~ coach_end
if not (quest.the_locket >= the_coach)
quest the_locket the_coach
endif
-> END

~ talk_after
if name:tamsin_tealeaf and (flag.locket_fate == "pool" or flag.locket_fate == "ireena")
-> after
endif
-> END

~ after
Narrator: For the first night since you met him, Tamsin falls asleep before anyone else, and doesn't wake once.
interject name:tamsin_tealeaf: Mm? No. No dreams. I dreamed about a very large sandwich. It was wonderful. It didn't call my name once.
-> END
